Article 4 Tiered Copyleft Obligations
------------------------------------------------------------------------

This article defines the open source obligations for
different usage scenarios. The obligations vary based
on how the Software is incorporated into a Derivative Work.

4.1 Static Linking — Full Copyleft
    (a) If you distribute a Derivative Work that
        incorporates the Software through Static Linking,
        the entire Derivative Work must be licensed
        under PARLZ and its Corresponding Source must
        be made public.
    (b) This is the strongest form of copyleft obligation
        under this license.

4.2 Dynamic Linking — Moderate Copyleft
    (a) If you distribute a Derivative Work that
        incorporates the Software through Dynamic
        Linking, the Derivative Work's own code may
        remain under a different license, provided:
        (i) the Software itself remains under PARLZ;
        (ii) any modifications to the Software are
             open sourced under PARLZ;
        (iii) the Derivative Work does not impose
              restrictions on the Software that
              conflict with this license.
    (b) This allows Dynamic Linking of PARLZ libraries
        into otherwise differently-licensed software,
        reducing friction for library usage.

4.3 Network Service — Light Copyleft
    (a) If you provide the Software or a Derivative
        Work as a Network Service, you must:
        (i) make the Corresponding Source of any
            modifications to the Software publicly
            available;
        (ii) not remove or alter the license notices.
    (b) You are not required to open source the
        entire service infrastructure, only
        modifications to the Software itself.

4.4 Internal Use — No Copyleft Obligation
    (a) If you use the Software or a Derivative Work
        solely for internal purposes and do not
        Distribute it or provide it as a Network
        Service, no copyleft obligation is triggered.
    (b) Internal use includes development, testing,
        staging, and production use within your
        organization.


